+## F: Front-end ablation (2026-11-26)
+
+The earlier ablation (`experiments/ablation/`) compared the released detector
+with a single-window detector (the 205-ms window in all three channels) that
+early stopping had ended after epoch 10 (best epoch 4), and found no benefit of
+the short windows. A breakdown benchmark of simulated chirp trains (1000 down to
+75~ms) and close fish pairs (0.5--20~Hz), with the single-window detector
+continued to the released detector's 16 epochs, shows where the windows differ:
+the short windows find more chirps that follow each other within ~150~ms
+(training data: at least 200~ms), and they do not help assignment between
+close fish (the single-window detector is slightly better there; its apparent
+deficit in the first ablation was training length). New: the Methods paragraph
+"Spectrograms" (end), one sentence in the Fig. S4 caption, Appendix 7
+(`sec:supp_frontend_ablation`) with Fig. S7 (`fig:frontend_ablation`). All new
+prose is in `#toreview[...]`, every number a `#stat` of namespace
+`v2_frontend_ablation`. Analysis: `paper/scripts/plot_v2_frontend_ablation.py`
+and `v2_fig_extract.py frontends` (chain `v2figs`); benchmark and report in
+`experiments/ablation_scenarios/` and
+`$CHIRP_DATA/runs/ablation_frontend/scenarios/REPORT.md`.
+
+- **F1 · Methods wording.** The "no measurable benefit" sentence (P3) is
+  replaced by the result; check depth (the share of chirps within 150~ms of a
+  same-fish chirp in the analyzed trials, 2.6%, could also go to the
+  Discussion or be dropped) and whether "for the same number of epochs" needs
+  the Appendix's explanation in the Methods.
+- **F2 · Placement.** Appendix 7 is last so that Figs. S1--S6 keep their
+  numbers; it could follow the failure modes (Appendix 4, Fig. S4) as Fig. S5,
+  which would renumber the chase-validation and random-effects figures.
+- **F3 · What the comparison does not cover.** One training run per front end
+  (seed variance unknown); simulation only, except the one real train of
+  Fig. S4C (62 vs 53 of 109 chirps); the simulator never lets the chirps of one
+  fish overlap, so its fastest trains are ~13 chirps/s while the real train
+  reaches 40-ms intervals; the recall difference depends on the threshold
+  (3.6 points at the operating threshold 0.8, 5.1 at 0.5) because the two
+  detectors' scores are calibrated differently (the single-window detector
+  scores isolated chirps a little higher: at 0.8 it finds as many or slightly
+  more chirps of slow trains, CIs including 0). Decide how much of this the
+  caption or the Appendix should state.
+
