+= Front-end ablation of the chirp detector <sec:supp_frontend_ablation>
+
+#toreview[The detector reads three spectrograms of the recording as the red,
+  green and blue channels of one image (#link(<met:chirp_detection>)[Methods]).
+  To test what the two short windows add, we trained a second detector with the
+  205-ms spectrogram in all three channels and otherwise identically (the same
+  simulated training scenes, seed and schedule). Early stopping had ended its
+  training after
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.training.single_early_epochs_run") epochs
+  (best epoch
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.training.single_early_best_epoch")), whereas the
+  released detector trained for
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.training.rgb_epochs_run") epochs (best epoch
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.training.rgb_best_epoch")) and improved its
+  emitter estimate only in the later ones; we therefore continued the
+  single-window detector's training on the same schedule and compared both at
+  epoch #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.training.single_epoch"). Both detectors ran
+  on a benchmark of simulated scenes with chirps of unseen fish on real
+  background recordings: chirp trains of two fish whose chirps followed each
+  other at intervals of
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.bench.rate.slowest_level_ms", digits: 0) down to
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.bench.rate.fastest_level_ms", digits: 0)~ms, and
+  pairs of fish
+  #stat("v2_frontend_ablation.bench.df.closest_level_hz", digits: 1)--#stat("v2_frontend_ablation.bench.df.widest_level_hz", digits: 0)~Hz
+  apart (@fig:frontend_ablation).]
+
